: \nL37:   * cite8†View PDF L38:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite14†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA mathematical perspective on Transformers (https://arxiv.org/html/2312.10794v2)\nciteturn2view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"pattern\":\"2.3.2\"}); Total lines: 1130\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract. L16:   2. cite6†1 Outline L17:   3. cite7†I Modeling L18:     1. cite8†2 Interacting particle system L19:       1. cite9†2.1 Residual neural networks L20:       2. cite10†2.2 The interacting particle system L21:       3. cite11†2.3 Toward the complete Transformer L22:         1. cite12†2.3.1 Multi-headed attention L23:         2. cite13†2.3.2 Feed-forward layers L24:     2. cite14†3 Measure to measure flow map L25:       1. cite15†3.1 The continuity equation L26:       2. cite16†3.2 The interaction energy L27:       3. cite17†3.3 A Wasserstein gradient flow proxy L107: We begin our discussion by presenting the mathematical model for a Transformer (Section cite8†2 ). We focus on a slightly simplified version that includes the self-attention mechanism as well as layer normalization, but excludes additional feed-forward layers commonly used in practice; see Section cite13†2.3.2 . This leads to a highly nonlinear mean-field interacting particle system.\nL133: A Transformer is then a flow map on $(\\mathbb{S}^{d-1})^{n}$: the input sequence $(x_{i}(0))_{i\\in[n]}\\in(\\mathbb{S}^{d-1})^{n}$ is an initial condition which is evolved through the dynamics\nL134: \nL135: (2.3)  |  | $$\\dot{x}_{i}(t)=\\mathbf{P}_{x_{i}(t)}\\left(\\frac{1}{Z_{\\beta,i}(t)}\\sum_{j=1}^{n}e^{\\beta\\langle Q(t)x_{i}(t),K(t)x_{j}(t)\\rangle}V(t)x_{j}(t)\\right)$$  |\nL136: \nL137: for all $i\\in[n]$ and $t\\geq 0$. Here and henceforth\nL138: \nL139:  | $$\\mathbf{P}_{x}y=y-\\langle x,y\\rangle x$$  |\nL144: The interacting particle system (cite63†2.3 )–(cite64†2.4 ), a simplified version of which was first written down in [cite65†LLH+20 , cite66†DGCC21 , cite67†SABP22 ], importantly contains the true novelty that Transformers carry with regard to other models: the self-attention mechanism\nL145: \nL146: (2.5)  |  | $$A_{ij}(t):=\\frac{e^{\\langle Q(t)x_{i}(t),K(t)x_{j}(t)\\rangle}}{Z_{\\beta,i}(t)}\\,,\\hskip 28.45274pt(i,j)\\in[n]^{2},$$  |\nL157: which is non-symmetric in general ($a_{ij}\\neq a_{ji}$), much like (cite63†2.3 ). When $\\phi$ is compactly supported, it has been shown in [cite73†JM14 ] that the particles $x_{i}(t)$ assemble in several clusters as $t\\rightarrow+\\infty$. Other related models include those of Vicsek [cite74†VCBJ+95 ], Hegselmann-Krause [cite75†HK02 ] and Cucker-Smale [cite76†CS07 ]. All these models exhibit a clustering behavior under various assumptions (see [cite77†MT14 , cite78†Tad23 ] and the references therein).\nL166: This feature is largely untapped in classical natural language processing applications, as the Transformer output is drawn from the distribution $\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\delta_{x_{i}(t)}$ so that the ordering of the output token is lost. There exists other applications, however where this property is used [cite82†BVE23 ].\nL167: #### 2.3. Toward the complete Transformer\nL168: \nL169: There are a couple of additional mechanisms used in practical implementations that we do not explicitly address or use in this study. The mathematical analysis of these mechanisms remains open.\nL170: ##### 2.3.1. Multi-headed attention\nL171: \nL172: Practical implementations spread out the computation of the self-attention mechanism at every $t$ through a sequence of heads, leading to the so-called multi-headed self attention. This consists in considering the following modification to (cite69†SA ):\nL173: \nL174: (2.7)  |  | $$\\dot{x}_{i}(t)=\\mathbf{P}_{x_{i}(t)}\\left(\\sum_{h=1}^{H}\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{e^{\\beta\\langle Q_{h}x_{i}(t),K_{h}x_{j}(t)\\rangle}}{Z_{\\beta,i,h}(t)}V_{h}x_{j}(t)\\right)$$  |\nL175: where $Z_{\\beta,i,h}(t)$ is defined as in (cite64†2.4 ) for the matrices $Q_{h}$ and $K_{h}$. The integer $H\\geq 1$ is called the number of heads^{5}^{5} 5 In practical implementations, $H$ is a divisor of $d$, and the query and key matrices $Q_{h}$ and $K_{h}$ are $\\frac{d}{H}\\times d$ rectangular. This allows for further parallelization of computations and increased expressiveness. For mathematical purposes, we focus on working with arbitrary integers $H$, and square weight matrices $Q_{h}$ and $K_{h}$..\nL176: The introduction of multiple heads also allows for drawing some interesting parallels with the literature on feed-forward neural networks, such as ResNets (cite59†2.1 ). Considerable effort has been expended to understand $2$-layer neural networks with width tending to $+\\infty$; more precisely, consider (cite59†2.1 ) with $L=1$, $w\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times\\ell}$, $a\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times d}$, and $\\ell\\to+\\infty$.\nL178: ###### Problem 1 (Approximation).\nL179: \nL180: Fix $d,n\\geq 2$ and consider the $1$-hidden layer Transformer with multi-headed self attention $f_{\\theta}^{H}:(\\mathbb{S}^{d-1})^{n}\\to(\\mathbb{S}^{d-1})^{n}$ defined as\nL181: \nL182:  | $$f_{\\theta}^{H}(x_{1},\\ldots,x_{n})_{i}=\\mathbf{P}_{x_{i}}\\left(\\sum_{h=1}^{H}\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{e^{\\beta\\langle Q_{h}x_{i},K_{h}x_{j}\\rangle}}{Z_{\\beta,i,h}}V_{h}x_{j}\\right),$$  |\nL183: where $H\\geq 1$ and $\\theta=(Q_{h},K_{h},V_{h})_{h\\in[H]}$ are as for (cite85†2.7 ). Can one approximate, in some appropriate topology, any continuous and permutation-equivariant function $f:(\\mathbb{S}^{d-1})^{n}\\to(\\mathbb{S}^{d-1})^{n}$ by means of some $f_{\\theta}^{H}$ as $H\\to+\\infty$? The same question for the multi-headed Transformer without layer normalization: $f_{\\theta}^{H}:(\\mathbb{R}^{d})^{n}\\to(\\mathbb{R}^{d})^{n}$ defined as\nL184:  | $$f_{\\theta}^{H}(x_{1},\\ldots,x_{n})_{i}=\\sum_{h=1}^{H}\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{e^{\\beta\\langle Q_{h}x_{i},K_{h}x_{j}\\rangle}}{Z_{\\beta,i,h}}V_{h}x_{j},$$  |\nL185: \nL186: is also open.\nL187: A universal approximation property of the above kind would then motivate studying the training dynamics of infinite-width (i.e., infinite number of heads) $1$-hidden layer Transformers, similar to what has been done for the neural network analog in recent years [cite86†CB18 , cite87†MMN18 , cite88†RVE22 ]. None of these questions has received a definitive answer for Transformers; see [cite89†YBR+19 ] for related work when the depth is taken to infinity.\nL188: ##### 2.3.2. Feed-forward layers\nL189: \nL190: The complete Transformer dynamics combines all of the above mechanisms with a feed-forward layer. This amounts to considering dynamics of the form\nL191: \nL192: (2.8)  |  | $$\\dot{x}_{i}(t)=\\mathbf{P}_{x_{i}(t)}\\left(w(t)\\sigma\\left(\\sum_{h=1}^{H}\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{e^{\\beta\\langle Q_{h}x_{i}(t),K_{h}x_{j}(t)\\rangle}}{Z_{\\beta,i,h(t)}}V_{h}x_{j}(t)+b(t)\\right)\\right),$$  |\nL193: where $w(t),b(t)$ and $\\sigma$ are as in (cite90†2.2 ). These layers are critical and drive the existing results on approximation properties of Transformers [cite89†YBR+19 ]. Nevertheless, the analysis of this model is beyond the scope of our current methods.\nL194: ### 3. Measure to measure flow map\nL195: An important aspect of Transformers is that they are not hard-wired to take into account the order of the input sequence, contrary to other architectures used for natural language processing such as recurrent neural networks. In these applications, each token $x_{i}(0)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ contains not only a word embedding $w_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, but also an additional positional encoding (we postpone a discussion to Remark cite91†3.2 ) which allows tokens to also carry their position in the input sequence.\nL196: Therefore, an input sequence is perfectly encoded as a set of tokens $\\{x_{1}(0),\\ldots,x_{n}(0)\\}$, or equivalently as the empirical measure of its constituent tokens $\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\delta_{x_{i}(0)}$. Recall that the output of a transformer is also a probability measure, namely $\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\delta_{x_{i}(t)}$, albeit one that captures the likelihood of the next token. As a result, one can view Transformers as flow maps between probability measures on $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$.\nL197: To describe this flow map, we appeal to the continuity equation, which governs precisely the evolution of the empirical measure of particles subject to dynamics.\nL198: #### 3.1. The continuity equation\nL199: \nL200: The vector field driving the evolution of a single particle in (cite69†SA ) clearly depends on all $n$ particles. In fact, one can equivalently rewrite the dynamics as\nL201: \nL202: (3.1)  |  | $$\\dot{x}_{i}(t)=\\mathcal{X}[\\mu(t)](x_{i}(t))$$  |\nL203: \nL204: for all $i\\in[n]$ and $t\\geq 0$, where\nL205: \nL206:  | $$\\mu(t,\\cdot)=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\delta_{x_{i}(t)}(\\cdot)$$  |\nL207: \nL208: is the empirical measure, while the vector field $\\mathcal{X}[\\mu]:\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}\\to\\mathrm{T}\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ reads\nL209: (3.2)  |  | $$\\mathcal{X}[\\mu](x)=\\mathbf{P}_{x}\\left(\\frac{1}{Z_{\\beta,\\mu}(x)}\\int e^{\\beta\\langle x,y\\rangle}y\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mu(y)\\right)$$  |\nL210: \nL211: with\nL212: \nL213: (3.3)  |  | $$Z_{\\beta,\\mu}(x)=\\int e^{\\beta\\langle x,y\\rangle}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mu(y).$$  |\nL214: In other words, (cite69†SA ) is a mean-field interacting particle system. The evolution of $\\mu(t)$ is governed by the continuity equation^{6}^{6} 6 Unless stated otherwise, $\\nabla$ and $\\mathrm{div}$ henceforth stand for the spherical gradient and divergence respectively, and all integrals are taken over $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$.\nL215: (3.4)  |  | $$\\begin{cases}\\partial_{t}\\mu+\\mathrm{div}(\\mathcal{X}[\\mu]\\mu)=0&\\text{ on }\\mathbb{R}_{\\geq 0}\\times\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}\\\\\nL216: \\mu_{|t=0}=\\mu(0)&\\text{ on }\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}\\end{cases}$$  |\nL217: \nL218: satisfied in the sense of distributions.\nL219: ###### Remark 3.1.\nL220: \nL221: Global existence of weak, measure-valued solutions to (cite92†3.4 ) for arbitrary initial conditions $\\mu(0)\\in\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{S}^{d-1})$ follows by arguing exactly as in [cite93†GLPR23 , Lemma A.3]. Here and henceforth, $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{S}^{d-1})$ stands for the set of Borel probability measures on $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$.\nL222: ###### Remark 3.2.\nL223: For the sake of completeness, in this brief segue we discuss a few ways to perform positional encoding. The original one, proposed in [cite42†VSP+17 ], proceeds as follows. Consider a sequence $(w_{i})_{i\\in[n]}\\in(\\mathbb{R}^{d})^{n}$ of word embeddings.\nL224: Then the positional encoding $p_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ of the $i$-th word embedding is defined as $(p_{i})_{2k}=\\sin(\\frac{i}{M^{2k/d}})$ and $(p_{i})_{2k+1}=\\cos(\\frac{i}{M^{2k/d}})$ for $k\\in[d/2-1]$, and $M>0$ is a user-defined scalar equal to $10^{4}$ in [cite42†VSP+17 ]. The $i$-th token is then defined as the addition: $x_{i}(0)=w_{i}+p_{i}$. Subsequent works simply use either a random^{7}^{7} 7 This rationale supports the assumption that initial tokens are drawn at random, which we make use of later on.\nL225: positional encoding (i.e., $p_{i}$ is just some random vector) or a trained transformation. The addition can also be replaced with a concatenation $x_{i}(0)=[w_{i};p_{i}]$. (See [cite94†LWLQ22 , cite95†XZ23 ] for details.)\nL233: #### 3.2. The interaction energy\nL234: \nL235: One can naturally ask whether the evolution in (cite92†3.4 ) admits some quantities which are monotonic when evaluated along the flow. As it turns out, the interaction energy\nL236: \nL237: (3.5)  |  | $$\\mathsf{E}_{\\beta}[\\mu]=\\frac{1}{2\\beta}\\iint e^{\\beta\\langle x,x^{\\prime}\\rangle}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mu(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mu(x^{\\prime})$$  |\nL238: \nL239: is one such quantity. Indeed,\nL273: For (cite92†3.4 ) to be the Wasserstein gradient flow of $\\mathsf{E}_{\\beta}$, the vector field $\\mathcal{X}[\\mu]$ defined in  (cite118†3.2 ) ought to be the gradient of the first variation $\\delta\\mathsf{E}_{\\beta}$ of $\\mathsf{E}_{\\beta}$. However, notice that $\\mathcal{X}[\\mu]$ is a logarithmic derivative:\nL274: \nL275: (3.7)  |  | $$\\mathcal{X}[\\mu](x)=\\nabla\\log\\int\\beta^{-1}e^{\\beta\\langle x,y\\rangle}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mu(y).$$  |\nL277: One is then naturally led to look for ways to \"symmetrize\" (cite92†3.4 ). A first attempt would be to remove the logarithm in (cite119†3.7 ), which amounts to removing the denominator in (cite118†3.2 ). This is one point where working on the unit sphere is useful: should one work on $\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, without layer normalization, the resulting vector field would grow exponentially with the size of the support of the measure, rendering a Cauchy-Lipschitz argument inapplicable.\nL304: We can actually write (cite123†3.9 ) more succinctly by recalling the definition of the convolution of two functions on $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ [cite113†DX13 , Chapter 2]: for any $g\\in L^{1}(\\mathbb{S}^{d-1})$ and $f:[-1,1]\\to\\mathbb{R}$ such that $t\\mapsto(1-t^{2})^{\\frac{d-3}{2}}f(t)$ is integrable,\nL305: \nL306:  | $$(f\\ast g)(x)=\\int f(\\langle x,y\\rangle)g(y)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\sigma_{d}(y).$$  |\nL358:  | $$\\|x_{i}(t)-x^{*}\\|\\leq Ce^{-\\lambda t}$$  |\nL359: \nL360: holds for all $i\\in[n]$ and $t\\geq 0$.\nL361: \nL362: In fact, let $Q$ and $K$ be arbitrary $d\\times d$ matrices. Then the same result also holds for the solution to the corresponding Cauchy problem for (cite63†2.3 ) with $V=I_{d}$ (or the natural analogue of (cite121†USA ) with these parameters).\nL363: \nL364: This is referred to as convergence toward consensus in collective behavior models.\nL370:  | $$\\|x_{i}(t)-x^{*}\\|\\leq Ce^{-\\lambda t}$$  |\nL371: \nL372: holds for all $i\\in[n]$ and $t\\geq 0$.\nL373: \nL374: In fact, let $Q$ and $K$ be arbitrary $d\\times d$ matrices. Then the same result also holds for the solution to the corresponding Cauchy problem for (cite63†2.3 ) with $V=I_{d}$ (or the natural analogue of (cite121†USA ) with these parameters).\nL552: Mimicking the proof strategy of [cite166†BCM15 , Theorem 2.2] and [cite167†HKR18 , Theorem 3.2], we sharpen this result by showing that the appearance of an antipodal particle is non-generic over the choice of initial conditions.\nL1041:   * [CLP15] Marco Caponigro, Anna Chiara Lai, and Benedetto Piccoli. A nonlinear model of opinion formation on the sphere. Discrete & Continuous Dynamical Systems-A, 35(9):4241–4268, 2015.\nL1042:   * [CLT20] Christa Cuchiero, Martin Larsson, and Josef Teichmann. Deep neural networks, generic universal interpolation, and controlled ODEs. SIAM Journal on Mathematics of Data Science, 2(3):901–919, 2020.\nL1064:   * [HKPZ16] Seung-Yeal Ha, Dongnam Ko, Jinyeong Park, and Xiongtao Zhang. Collective synchronization of classical and quantum oscillators. EMS Surveys in Mathematical Sciences, 3(2):209–267, 2016.\nL1065:   * [HKR18] Seung-Yeal Ha, Dongnam Ko, and Sang Woo Ryoo. On the relaxation dynamics of Lohe oscillators on some Riemannian manifolds. Journal of Statistical Physics, 172:1427–1478, 2018.\nL1066:   * [HR17] Eldad Haber and Lars Ruthotto. Stable architectures for deep neural networks. Inverse problems, 34(1), 2017.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSimilarity of Neural Network Representations Revisited (https://proceedings.mlr.press/v97/kornblith19a.html)\nciteturn2view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"pattern\":\"Abstract\"}); Total lines: 50\nL0: Proceedings of Machine Learning Research\nL1: \nL2: [Input]\nL3: \nL4: cite0†Volume 97 cite1†JMLR†www.jmlr.org cite2†DMLR†data.mlr.press cite3†TMLR†jmlr.org cite4†MLOSS†www.jmlr.org cite5†FAQ cite6†Submission Format L5: \nL6: [cite7†edit†github.com ]\nL7: \nL8: # Similarity of Neural Network Representations Revisited\nL9: \nL10: Simon Kornblith, Mohammad Norouzi, Honglak Lee, Geoffrey Hinton\nL11: \nL12: Proceedings of the 36th International Conference on Machine Learning, PMLR 97:3519-3529, 2019.\nL13: #### Abstract\nL14: Recent work has sought to understand the behavior of neural networks by comparing representations between layers and between different trained models. We examine methods for comparing neural network representations based on canonical correlation analysis (CCA).\nL15: We show that CCA belongs to a family of statistics for measuring multivariate similarity, but that neither CCA nor any other statistic that is invariant to invertible linear transformation can measure meaningful similarities between representations of higher dimension than the number of data points. We introduce a similarity index that measures the relationship between representational similarity matrices and does not suffer from this limitation.\nL16: This similarity index is equivalent to centered kernel alignment (CKA) and is also closely connected to CCA. Unlike CCA, CKA can reliably identify correspondences between representations in networks trained from different initializations.\n"
